// @emit-error emitProgram: struct equality is not supported yet
// THE SECOND DISAGREEMENT SHAPE, and the one D57's first fix did not reach: two DIFFERENT
// arms of ONE union, same layout, compared to each other. On master this was check-clean
// INVALID WASM with D57's own signature (`type mismatch: expected i32, found (ref $type)`) —
// both operands resolved, to two DIFFERENT variant rows, so the agreement rung declines;
// neither has an `sNames` row, so the refusal gate — which asked only the struct table —
// was false; and the compare fell into the i32 tail at the bottom of the binary lowering.
//
// THE WIN DIRECTION HERE IS A REFUSAL, NOT `runs`, AND THE CONTROL SAYS SO. Delete
// `type Shape` and `Circle == Ext` is two ordinary struct rows that disagree, which is
// exactly `emitProgram: struct equality is not supported yet`. D57's claim is that a union's
// mere presence must not change the outcome; for THIS pair the outcome to match is the loud
// one, and matching it is what closes the row rather than widening D45 — nothing that ran
// stops running, a silent cell becomes the same diagnostic its no-union twin already gave.
//
// (Two same-layout arms of one union have two heap types that this emitter does not prove
// equal, so comparing through either row would be a guess. If `uVarTwin` is ever taught to
// collapse them, this file is where the change shows up.)
type Circle = { r: i32 }
type Ext = { r: i32 }
type Sq = { s: i32 }
type Shape = Circle | Ext | Sq

function cell() {
  const a: Circle = { r: 1 }
  const e: Ext = { r: 1 }
  return a == e
}

print(cell())
